 "subs": [
  [
   1.6,
   4.46,
   "A banana. A piece of tape.",
   "1"
  ],
  [
   4.72,
   8.54,
   "$6,240,000.",
   "1"
  ],
  [
   8.68,
   14.42,
   "That's what Maurizio Cattelan's Comedian\nsold for at Sotheby's in November 2024.",
   "1"
  ],
  [
   14.91,
   18.43,
   "So how did we get from Van Gogh painting the night sky\u2026",
   "1"
  ],
  [
   18.48,
   21.82,
   "\u2026to millions for fruit on a wall?",
   "1"
  ],
  [
   22.1,
   27.31,
   "Once, art mostly meant skill, observation and imagination.",
   "2"
  ],
  [
   27.82,
   32.81,
   "Van Gogh turned the view from his asylum window\ninto a sky that moves.",
   "2"
  ],
  [
   33.16,
   38.34,
   "Picasso broke the human figure apart,\nand showed it from several sides at once.",
   "2"
  ],
  [
   38.45,
   43.73,
   "In Guernica, he turned the bombing of a town\ninto a cry against war.",
   "2"
  ],
  [
   44.3,
   48.12,
   "But the real break came twenty years before Guernica.",
   "3"
  ],
  [
   48.51,
   53.28,
   "In 1917, Marcel Duchamp signed a urinal with a fake name,",
   "3"
  ],
  [
   53.33,
   57.66,
   "and sent it to an exhibition that promised to show everything.",
   "3"
  ],
  [
   57.71,
   59.92,
   "They hid it behind a partition.",
   "3"
  ],
  [
   60.04,
   64.75,
   "A small magazine defended it in three words.\nHe chose it.",
   "3"
  ],
  [
   64.8,
   69.14,
   "What if the artist's idea mattered more\nthan the artist's hand?",
   "3"
  ],
  [
   69.4,
   76.04,
   "In 1964, Andy Warhol showed boxes\nthat looked just like supermarket cartons.",
   "4"
  ],
  [
   76.09,
   80.86,
   "A philosopher asked why those were art,\nand the ones in the stockroom weren't.",
   "4"
  ],
  [
   80.91,
   83.35,
   "His answer: an artworld.",
   "4"
  ],
  [
   83.63,
   87.0,
   "By the late sixties, art could be an instruction.",
   "4"
  ],
  [
   87.05,
   89.81,
   "An ordinary object. A question.",
   "4"
  ],
  [
   90.5,
   92.98,
   "Fast-forward to 2021.",
   "5"
  ],
  [
   93.32,
   99.24,
   "Salvatore Garau sold a sculpture called Io Sono\nfor \u20ac15,000.",
   "5"
  ],
  [
   99.5,
   101.3,
   "It is invisible.",
   "5"
  ],
  [
   102.27,
   105.51,
   "The buyer got a certificate, and instructions:",
   "5"
  ],
  [
   105.7,
   110.32,
   "display it in a space five feet square, free of obstructions.",
   "5"
  ],
  [
   110.4,
   112.67,
   "Yes. Empty space.",
   "5"
  ],
  [
   113.6,
   115.66,
   "And then, the banana.",
   "6"
  ],
  [
   115.85,
   120.48,
   "But here's the detail.\nThe collector wasn't really paying for fruit.",
   "6"
  ],
  [
   120.7,
   124.74,
   "Comedian comes with a certificate of authenticity\nand instructions.",
   "6"
  ],
  [
   124.79,
   128.0,
   "The banana itself is meant to be replaced.",
   "6"
  ],
  [
   128.15,
   134.11,
   "The one at Sotheby's was reportedly bought\nfrom a fruit stand outside. For 35 cents.",
   "6"
  ],
  [
   134.23,
   138.58,
   "So what was the buyer buying?\nAn idea? A symbol?",
   "6"
  ],
  [
   138.63,
   143.97,
   "Or proof that prestige, scarcity and publicity\ncan create a price?",
   "6"
